    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-emg-geometric-scaling",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "SI and geometrized Einstein–Maxwell parameters",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#def-emg-geometric-scaling; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C0",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A definition; explicit SI and geometric parameter dictionary",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Use the smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, and arbitrary smooth real two-form $F$ of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. In a chart using the coordinate convention $x^0=ct$ with all four coordinates assigned metres, assign the SI component unit tesla to $F_{ab}$; the two-form $F=\\frac12F_{ab}\\,dx^a\\wedge dx^b$ then has unit weber.\n\nFor this conversion dictionary, take $c>0$, $G>0$, and $\\mu_0>0$ as numerical input parameters, with SI dimensions\n\n$$[c]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}},\\qquad [G]=\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad [\\mu_0]=\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,C^{-2}}.$$\n\nDefine the derived parameters\n\n$$\\varepsilon_0:=\\frac{1}{\\mu_0c^2},\\qquad \\kappa:=\\frac{8\\pi G}{c^4},\\qquad \\alpha:=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\kappa}{2\\mu_0}},\\qquad \\mathcal F:=\\alpha F.$$\n\nThus $\\varepsilon_0$ has unit $\\mathrm{C^2\\,s^2\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$, $\\kappa$ has unit $\\mathrm{m\\,J^{-1}}$, $\\alpha$ has unit $\\mathrm{C\\,s\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$, and the components $\\mathcal F_{ab}$ have unit $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. In modern SI, $c$ is fixed exactly by the definition of the metre; $\\mu_0$ is not fixed exactly and its numerical value is determined experimentally. Here the equations use only the positive symbolic parameters above, not a selected numerical value.\n\nFor a physical mass $M_{\\!p}>0$ in kilograms, signed charge $Q_e$ in coulombs, and angular momentum $J_{\\mathrm{ang}}$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$, define the geometric length parameters\n\n$$m_{\\mathrm{geo}}:=\\frac{GM_{\\!p}}{c^2},\\qquad q_{\\mathrm{geo}}:=\\sqrt{\\frac{G}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^4}}\\,Q_e,\\qquad a_{\\mathrm{geo}}:=\\frac{J_{\\mathrm{ang}}}{M_{\\!p}c}.$$\n\nFor a supplied magnetic-flux parameter $\\Phi_B$ in webers, also define $p_{\\mathrm{geo}}:=\\alpha\\Phi_B/(4\\pi)$ in metres. This is only a conversion of an already specified flux value; it asserts neither a global potential nor a magnetic source. The inverse parameter maps are $M_{\\!p}=c^2m_{\\mathrm{geo}}/G$, $Q_e=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^4/G}\\,q_{\\mathrm{geo}}$, $J_{\\mathrm{ang}}=M_{\\!p}c\\,a_{\\mathrm{geo}}$, and $\\Phi_B=4\\pi p_{\\mathrm{geo}}/\\alpha$.\n\nThe mass, charge, angular momentum and flux are inputs to this dictionary; their primitive or derived status in any physical model is framework-specific. No field equation, Maxwell postulate, matter law, or empirical value is part of this definition.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "BIPM, The International System of Units (SI Brochure), 9th edition, version 4.01, §2.3 and the ampere definition, printed pp. 124 and 129",
              "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4, author-hosted full-text HTML, Einstein–Maxwell normalization and stress conventions",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§4.3–4.5 and 7.2, Maxwell forms, Einstein equations and electromagnetic stress",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Exact geometric electrovac normalization",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C0",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; complete exact trace and normalization argument",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$, $F$, $\\kappa$, $\\mu_0$, $\\alpha$ and $\\mathcal F=\\alpha F$ be as in [[def-emg-geometric-scaling]], with curvature tensors defined by [[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]. Let $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ be a constant. Define the quadratic tensor\n\n$$\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F):=F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\frac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd},$$\n\nwhere indices are raised with $g^{-1}$. Then the following equations for the same metric and two-form are equivalent:\n\n$$G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F),$$\n\n$$G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=2\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,\\mathcal F),$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}(g)-\\Lambda g_{ab}=2\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,\\mathcal F).$$\n\nThese are algebraic equivalences between tensor equations. No Maxwell equation, matter model, limiting procedure or physical solution is assumed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4, author-hosted full-text HTML, Maxwell action, Einstein equation and electromagnetic stress tensor",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§3.4–3.5, 4.3–4.5 and 7.2, curvature contractions, Maxwell equations and electromagnetic stress tensor",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Use quadratic homogeneity to change the electromagnetic normalization, then take the four-dimensional trace and reverse the trace equation.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-emg-conformal-maxwell",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Four-dimensional conformal Maxwell covariance",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#thm-emg-conformal-maxwell; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C1",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; complete conditional Hodge, current and stress transformations",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ and a smooth real two-form $F$ be as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Let $\\Omega:M\\to(0,\\infty)$ be a smooth dimensionless function, put $\\widetilde g=\\Omega^2g$ with the same orientation, let $\\mu_0>0$ be constant, and let $j$ be a smooth vector field. The Hodge operator is that of [[lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier]]. Define\n\n$$\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F):=F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\frac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd},\\qquad T_{ab}(g,F):=\\mu_0^{-1}\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F).$$\n\nThen $(F,j)$ satisfies the differential-form Maxwell equations\n\n$$dF=0,\\qquad d(\\star_gF)=\\mu_0\\,\\iota_j\\operatorname{vol}_g$$\n\nif and only if $(F,\\widetilde j)$ satisfies the same equations for $\\widetilde g$, where $\\widetilde j:=\\Omega^{-4}j$. In addition,\n\n$$\\operatorname{vol}_{\\widetilde g}=\\Omega^4\\operatorname{vol}_g,\\qquad \\iota_{\\widetilde j}\\operatorname{vol}_{\\widetilde g}=\\iota_j\\operatorname{vol}_g,\\qquad \\widetilde T_{ab}=\\Omega^{-2}T_{ab},\\qquad \\widetilde T^{ab}=\\Omega^{-6}T^{ab}.$$\n\nIf $n$ is a $g$-unit timelike vector, then $\\widetilde n:=\\Omega^{-1}n$ is $\\widetilde g$-unit timelike and $\\widetilde T(\\widetilde n,\\widetilde n)=\\Omega^{-4}T(n,n)$. On any oriented integration domain where the integral exists, or for a compactly supported action difference, the quadratic integral $\\int F_{ab}F^{ab}\\operatorname{vol}_g$ is unchanged. These are tensor identities; they do not assert Einstein-equation or matter-model invariance.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §3.1.3, author-hosted full-text HTML, Hodge dual",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S3.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4.5.1, author-hosted full-text HTML, Maxwell field equation and action",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§3.1–3.2 and 4.3–4.5, Hodge dual, Maxwell forms and electromagnetic stress",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Apply the degree-two Hodge multiplier, determinant volume scaling, contraction linearity, and the explicit metric scalings of the quadratic tensor.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-emg-conformal-einstein-transformation",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Conformal Einstein tensor transformation",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#thm-emg-conformal-einstein-transformation; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C2",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; complete connection-difference and curvature-contraction proof with explicit external mathematical prerequisites",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be the smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]], with the curvature convention and tensors of [[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]. Let $\\Omega:M\\to(0,\\infty)$ be smooth and dimensionless, and put $\\widetilde g=\\Omega^2g$ with the same orientation. Use the metric-selected connection and its tensor derivative of [[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]] and [[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]. Define the smooth one-form and its contractions by\n\n$$u:=\\Omega^{-1}d\\Omega,\\qquad H_{ab}:=(\\nabla_a u)_b,\\qquad \\theta:=g^{ab}H_{ab},\\qquad s:=g^{ab}u_au_b.$$\n\nThen $H_{ab}=H_{ba}$ and\n\n$$\\widetilde{\\operatorname{Ric}}_{ab}=\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-2H_{ab}-g_{ab}\\theta+2u_au_b-2g_{ab}s,$$\n\n$$\\widetilde S=\\Omega^{-2}(S-6\\theta-6s),$$\n\n$$\\widetilde G_{ab}=G_{ab}-2H_{ab}+2u_au_b+2g_{ab}\\theta+g_{ab}s.$$\n\nAll curvature conventions are those stated in [[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]. These are mathematical transformation identities, not an assertion that the Einstein equation is conformally invariant.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4, author-hosted full-text HTML, conformal metric and Einstein equations",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§3.3–3.5 and 7.2, curvature contractions, conformal compactification and Einstein–Maxwell equations",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Compute the conformal connection difference, contract its curvature difference, expand both quadratic contractions, and trace to the scalar and Einstein tensors.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-emg-constant-electrovac-rescaling",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Constant coupled electrovac rescaling",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#prop-emg-constant-electrovac-rescaling; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C3",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; complete constant coupled electrovac and source rescaling",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "thm-emg-conformal-maxwell",
          "thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-emg-conformal-maxwell": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $g,F,j,\\Lambda,c,G,\\mu_0,\\kappa$ be as in [[def-emg-geometric-scaling]] and the four-dimensional Lorentzian curvature convention of [[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]. Suppose they satisfy the formal sourced Einstein–Maxwell equations\n\n$$G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F),\\qquad dF=0,\\qquad d(\\star_gF)=\\mu_0\\,\\iota_j\\operatorname{vol}_g,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal Q$ is the quadratic tensor of [[thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence]]. For a positive dimensionless constant $\\lambda$, define\n\n$$g':=\\lambda^2g,\\qquad F':=\\lambda F,\\qquad \\Lambda':=\\lambda^{-2}\\Lambda,\\qquad j':=\\lambda^{-3}j.$$\n\nThen $(g',F',j',\\Lambda')$ satisfies the same equations with the same numerical constants $c,G,\\mu_0$. In particular, a source-free electrovac solution ($j=0$) maps to a source-free electrovac solution. No transformation law for an additional matter stress tensor or matter action is asserted.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4, author-hosted full-text HTML, Maxwell equations, Einstein equation and electromagnetic stress",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§3.4–3.5, 4.3–4.5 and 7.2, curvature, Maxwell forms and coupled Einstein–Maxwell equations",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Use the Christoffel formula to preserve curvature, verify quadratic stress and cosmological scaling, then combine Maxwell covariance with linearity.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-emg-test-field-residual-order",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Exact small-field coupled residual order",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/integrator-inventory.json#prop-emg-test-field-residual-order; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/einstein-maxwell-models/complete-local-arguments.md#C4",
        "design_state": "reserved-EMG-C01 A result; exact fixed-background residual only, no solution-error theorem",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-geometric-scaling": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $U$ be a smooth Lorentzian four-dimensional chart domain with metric $g$ as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]], and let $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ be constant. Write $\\Omega^k(U)$ for the smooth real $k$-forms on $U$. Here $d:\\Omega^k(U)\\to\\Omega^{k+1}(U)$, $0\\le k\\le3$, is the exterior derivative with coordinate formula $(d\\beta)_{a_0\\ldots a_k}=(k+1)\\partial_{[a_0}\\beta_{a_1\\ldots a_k]}$, and $\\star_g:\\Omega^2(U)\\to\\Omega^2(U)$ is the Lorentzian Hodge map characterized by $\\alpha\\wedge\\star_g\\beta=\\frac12\\alpha_{ab}\\beta^{ab}\\,\\operatorname{vol}_g$ for $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Omega^2(U)$, as in [[lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus]]. Both operators are real-linear. Suppose $G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}=0$. Let $F_1\\in\\Omega^2(U)$ satisfy $dF_1=0$ and $d(\\star_gF_1)=0$. For any real dimensionless $\\varepsilon$, put $F_\\varepsilon:=\\varepsilon F_1$ and define\n\n$$T^{(1)}_{ab}:=\\mu_0^{-1}\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F_1),\\qquad \\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F;\\Lambda):=G_{ab}(g)+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}\\mathcal Q_{ab}(g,F),$$\n\nusing $\\kappa,\\mu_0,\\mathcal Q$ of [[def-emg-geometric-scaling]] and [[thm-emg-field-normalization-equivalence]]. Then $F_\\varepsilon$ remains an exact source-free Maxwell field on the fixed metric and\n\n$$\\mathscr R_{ab}(g,F_\\varepsilon;\\Lambda)=-\\kappa\\varepsilon^2T^{(1)}_{ab}.$$\n\nFor a smooth covariant two-tensor $S$ and compact $K\\subset U$, define the chart component norm of its restriction by $\\|S\\|_{K}:=\\max_{0\\le a,b\\le3}\\sup_{p\\in K}|S_{ab}(p)|$ when $K\\ne\\varnothing$. In this estimate and its applications, use $\\sup_{p\\in\\varnothing}f(p):=0$ for nonnegative component functions $f$; equivalently, the empty restriction has $\\|S\\|_{\\varnothing}:=0$. It is finite for smooth $S$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\|\\mathscr R(g,F_\\varepsilon;\\Lambda)\\|_K=\\kappa\\varepsilon^2\\|T^{(1)}\\|_K.$$\n\nIf $\\varepsilon\\ne0$ and $T^{(1)}$ is not identically zero on $K$, the fixed pair $(g,F_\\varepsilon)$ is not an exact coupled Einstein–Maxwell solution on $K$. This is a residual estimate only; it gives no nearby metric, solution-distance bound, point-source solution or backreaction limit.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, §4, author-hosted full-text HTML, Maxwell equations and quadratic electromagnetic stress",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S4.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1, §§4.3–4.5 and 7.2, Maxwell equations and electromagnetic stress tensor",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "Use linearity of source-free Maxwell equations and quadratic homogeneity of stress to compute the fixed-metric Einstein residual exactly.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
